   212	For initial sizing, use **ESTIMATE `C_H = 50–150` Midnight rows**, including the chosen domain encoding and selection work. A depth-32 read then costs **ESTIMATE 1,600–4,800 rows**, and a write **ESTIMATE 3,200–9,600 rows**. These are experiment inputs, not measurements of the installed Poseidon implementation. The implementation packs partial rounds, so generic Poseidon round counts cannot substitute for a row probe. [`C:hash/poseidon/poseidon_chip.rs:45`]
   213	
   214	Offline memory arguments must establish read-after-write consistency, initialization and finalization across the entire execution. Jolt’s memory treatment provides a concrete timestamp-based reference. Twist and Shout provide a different sumcheck-based route, with separate read/write and read-only arguments. [Jolt](https://eprint.iacr.org/2023/1217.pdf), [Twist and Shout](https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/105.pdf)
   215	
   216	For offline checking, derive challenges after committing the relevant records. Do not expose a challenge and then allow unconstrained future records to cancel an earlier inconsistency.
   217	
   218	### Inputs and outcomes
   219	
   220	Use a canonical typed input encoding. Authenticate input reads against `input_d`; proving only a host-computed input hash is insufficient.
   221	
   222	Define:
   223	
   224	```text
   225	Halted:
   226	    outcome_d = H("Halted", type_d, canonical_value_encoding_d)
   227	
   228	Rejected:
   229	    outcome_d = H("Rejected", canonical_error_code)
   230	
   231	Exhausted:
   232	    outcome_d = H("Exhausted", canonical_exhaustion_kind)
   233	```
   234	
   235	Large structured values are validated and hashed incrementally. Their size changes proving work, not public-statement width.
   236	
   237	The current witness stream accepts only halted UInt values, and the decider opens only a halted u128 result. Both need replacement. [`crates/mori-backend/src/sherlock_c6_witness_stream.rs:138`; `crates/mori-recursion/src/run_decider.rs:278`]
   238	
   239	Maintain separate concepts:
   240	
   241	- **Owner-step height:** range-checked below `2^64`, as GP-1 requires.
   242	- **Processor progress:** includes memory paths, parsing, hashing and verifier operations.
   243	
   244	An unfinished memory check must not consume an extra Sherlock owner step or publish an outcome. Processor continuation machinery must not introduce a smaller run-length limit.
   245	
   246	## 3. Foreign-key registry and statement adapters
   247	
   248	### Proposed registry entry
   249	
   250	Authenticate the complete verification interpretation:
   251	
   252	```text
   253	RegistryEntry = (
   254	    vk_id,
   255	    raw_vk_digest,
   256	    normalized_vk_digest,
   257	    proof_system_id,
   258	    proof_format_version,
   259	    curve_id,
   260	    commitment_scheme_id,
   261	    srs_id,
   262	    transcript_id,
   263	    constraint_system_descriptor_d,
   264	    public_input_schema_d,
   265	    verifier_program_d,
   266	    adapter_program_d,
   267	    claim_kind,
   268	    policy_epoch
   269	)
   270	```
   271	
   272	The contract authorizes a registry root or an authenticated registry-history root. The node proves entry membership against that root. The prover cannot select an arbitrary root and thereby authorize its own key.
   273	
   274	Admission should validate:
   275	
   276	1. Canonical key parsing and complete byte consumption.
   277	2. Agreement between raw bytes and the normalized verifier description.
   278	3. The curve, commitment scheme, transcript and SRS.
   279	4. The adapter’s input schema and allowed output claim kind.
   280	5. The admission authority’s policy.
   281	
   282	Use the same processor to prove normalization when it is too expensive to repeat. The admission certificate must bind both representations. A host-produced normalized key is not sufficient.
   283	
   284	Register the Sherlock processor/decider relations once. Individual Sherlock programs remain data and require no registry key entry.
   285	
   286	### Why the existing “public key” API is insufficient
   287	
   288	`assign_vk_as_public_input` assigns the transcript representative as a public input, but receives the evaluation domain and constraint system as host-side parameters. Those remain fixed by circuit construction. It is not an arbitrary-key verifier. [`C:verifier/verifier_gadget.rs:204`]
   289	
   290	The gadget also documents assumptions about challenge phases and instance columns. The native verifier has broader phase-processing logic. A registry cannot erase that difference. [`C:verifier/verifier_gadget.rs:14`; `P:plonk/verifier.rs:78`]
   291	
   292	For arbitrary admitted architectures, execute the verifier description as authenticated data in the processor. Its loops, expression evaluations, transcript parsing and MSM construction can span chunks. This keeps key size and architecture complexity out of the node key.
   293	
   294	A uniform-architecture fast path is useful, but it cannot be the only GP-3 path. The existing finite-family option explicitly fixes architecture and k. [`D:design.md:95`]
   295	
   296	### Compact proof compatibility
   297	
   298	A Compact contract proof **can be verified by a newly implemented in-circuit verifier**, but it cannot simply be passed to the current Poseidon recursive gadget.
   299	
   300	The exact compatibility requirements are:
   301	
   302	| Boundary | Source fact | Required implementation |
   303	|---|---|---|
   304	| Proof envelope | Ledger proof tag is `proof[v5]`; the payload is bytes. [`L:transient-crypto/src/proofs.rs:131`] | Parse the correct payload and version. |
   305	| Key envelope | Ledger key tag is `verifier-key[v7]`. [`L:transient-crypto/src/proofs.rs:370`] | Bind the complete registered key identity. |
   306	| Key structure | `MidnightVK` contains architecture, k, public-input count and the underlying PLONK key. Deserialization reconstructs the constraint system from the architecture. [`Z:lib.rs:1537`; `Z:lib.rs:1573`] | Authenticate architecture and reconstructed verifier semantics. |
   307	| Transcript | Ledger verification uses `blake2b_simd::State`. Recursive BLS verification uses `PoseidonState`. [`L:transient-crypto/src/proofs.rs:64`; `C:verifier/types.rs:140`] | Implement the actual Blake2b transcript. Reproving the circuit with Poseidon does not verify the supplied Compact proof. |
   308	| Blake2b details | The transcript uses a 64-byte keyed hash initialization, common-message prefixes and challenge prefixes. [`P:transcript/implementors.rs:13`] | Match byte encodings, prefixes, challenge extraction and field reduction exactly. |
   309	| VK transcript representative | The representative hashes commitments, domain and pinned constraint-system information. [`P:plonk/mod.rs:246`] | Bind this representative to the registered descriptor, rather than accepting an unrelated scalar. |
   310	| Public inputs | Ledger verification passes one ordinary public-input vector and no explicit committed instance; stdlib substitutes the identity commitment. [`L:transient-crypto/src/proofs.rs:563`; `Z:lib.rs:2020`] | Reproduce instance counts, lengths and committed-instance treatment. |
   311	| Complete parsing | Native verification rejects unused proof bytes; ledger key parsing rejects trailing key bytes. [`Z:utils/plonk_api.rs:144`; `L:transient-crypto/src/proofs.rs:532`] | Match these checks when claiming byte-level ledger compatibility. |
   312	
   313	There is a Blake2b chip wrapper in zk-stdlib. It is useful implementation material, but its existence does not make the recursive transcript compatible. [`Z:external/blake2b.rs:37`]
   314	
   315	The recursive transcript gadget also leaves subgroup checks out of point assignment and explicitly does not prove that every proof byte was consumed. Those choices must not be copied into a byte-exact foreign-proof adapter without a separate argument. [`C:verifier/transcript_gadget.rs:76`; `C:verifier/transcript_gadget.rs:123`]
   316	
   317	### Statement mapping
   318	
   319	ZKIR v3’s public statement is not simply its function arguments and return values. The circuit first publishes a binding input, optionally a communications commitment, and then guarded `Impact` values. Typed inputs and outputs enter the communications commitment when enabled. [`L:zkir-v3/src/ir_vm.rs:801`; `L:zkir-v3/src/ir_vm.rs:869`; `L:zkir-v3/src/ir_vm.rs:1173`]
   320	
   321	Provide two adapter levels:
   322	
   323	- **Generic circuit claim:** `CircuitSatisfied(vk_id, public_inputs_d)`.
   324	- **Typed application claim:** selected public fields, or values whose communications-commitment opening is checked in-circuit, mapped to a declared claim schema.
   325	
   326	Do not manufacture a public return value from an opaque communications commitment. If the adapter requires that value, it needs a valid opening or an explicitly exposed public value.
   327	
   328	A verified contract circuit proof establishes circuit satisfaction. It does not alone establish that a transaction was included on-chain or that its ledger effects were accepted. The ledger separately constructs transaction-bound inputs and validates calls. [`L:ledger/src/verify.rs:2119`; `L:ledger/src/verify.rs:2151`]
   329	
   330	“Same curve and commitment scheme” must not silently become “same transcript, same SRS and same gate layout.” Registry metadata selects the correct verifier program. Unrecognized variants require an admitted verifier implementation, not a new recursion key.
   331	
   332	## 4. PCD shape, DAG fan-out and formal properties
   333	
   334	### Proposed typed composition
   335	
   336	Use distinct claim kinds:
   337	
   338	```text
   339	Continuation
   340	RunSegment
   341	RunClaim
   342	ForeignCircuitClaim
   343	BatchClaim
   344	SettlementClaim
   345	```
   346	
   347	A segment join requires the same run identity, matching state boundaries and adjacent owner heights.
   348	
   349	A batch join permits different programs and inputs.
   350	
   351	A consumer edge binds a producer claim to a specified input position of another run. It checks the producer certificate and the adapter, rather than merely comparing two host-supplied digests.
   352	
   353	Incomplete continuations cannot be consumed as completed results or settled on-chain.
   354	
   355	### Bind shape inside the proved statement
   356	
   357	Propose a fixed-width node statement:
   358	
   359	```text
   360	NodeStatement = (
   361	    self_vk_repr,
   362	    policy_d,
   363	    message_d,
   364	    claim_set_d,
   365	    shape_d,
   366	    accumulator_encoding
   367	)
   368	```
   369	
   370	The opened message contains the mode-specific fields and processor continuation.
   371	
   372	Compute a shape commitment from typed, ordered edges:
   373	
   374	```text
   375	shape_d = H(
   376	    "SherlockPCDShape/v1",
   377	    node_kind,
   378	    enabled_slot_bitmap,
   379	    left_certificate_id,
   380	    right_certificate_id,
   381	    dependency_mapping_d,
   382	    local_work_d,
   383	    output_message_d
   384	)
   385	```
   386	
   387	A disabled slot has one canonical identifier. Swapping children changes the shape. A shared producer has one certificate identity referenced by both consumers.
   388	
   389	Keep semantic claim identity separate from proof-shape identity. Different aggregation schedules may prove the same semantic claim while having different shape commitments. Proof randomness must not create a new semantic run identity.
   390	
   391	The current Quint model explicitly makes shape binding a property of the decider **plus off-chain manifest replay**. The decider does not read that manifest. That does not establish GP-4’s in-statement shape guarantee. [`models/sherlock/quint/aggregation.qnt:18`; `models/sherlock/quint/aggregation.qnt:223`]
   392	
   393	### DAG reuse and exact coverage
   394	
   395	Permit a verified claim to feed several consumers. For example:
   396	
   397	```text
   398	        A
   399	       / \
   400	      B   C
   401	       \ /
   402	        D
   403	```
   404	
   405	`B` and `C` each authenticate `A`. `D` authenticates both consumers. The claim collection contains `A` once, while the dependency graph contains both outgoing edges.
   406	
   407	Do not sum child claim counts or financial effects without checking overlap. Propose a canonical authenticated map keyed by semantic claim identity. Insertion is idempotent for the same claim and rejects conflicting contents under the same identity.
   408	
   409	Exact set union can require substantial work. Prove it as a streamed processor computation over the authenticated child collections. A hash of two set roots does not by itself prove a canonical, duplicate-free union. A partially completed union stays a `Continuation`.
   410	
   411	Duplicate verification obligations are different from duplicate semantic effects. The recursive proof may authenticate a shared ancestor along both branches. That does not authorize counting its transfer or settlement twice.
   412	
   413	### Extend the formal models
   414	
   415	The existing Lean `link` requires equal programs and contiguous state/height boundaries. Its certificate datatype is a tree. Those definitions remain useful for **within-run segment composition**, but are not a model of cross-program DAG composition. [`clean/fork/Clean/Sherlock/Aggregation.lean:49`; `clean/fork/Clean/Sherlock/Aggregation.lean:72`]
   416	
   417	Preserve and extend the properties as follows:
   418	
   419	| Property | Proposed GP form |
   420	|---|---|
   421	| Unique genesis | Exactly one canonical genesis **per run identity**. A batch can contain many distinct runs and therefore many genesis states. |
   422	| Exactly-once execution | For each run, enabled owner transitions tile its claimed interval without gaps or repeated positions. |
   423	| DAG fan-out | Repeated references to a verified claim are allowed. Each reference has an explicit consumer and input position. |
   424	| Exact batch coverage | The published claim set is the proven union of the selected claims, with duplicates handled canonically. |
   425	| Replay prevention | Settlement effects are accepted once under a semantic nullifier policy. Rebracketing the proof cannot evade replay protection. |
   426	| Shape binding | Every accepted root commits to its typed edges and local work. It is no longer conditional on an external manifest checker. |
   427	| Fixed keys | No key depends on program, memory size, registry population, graph shape or depth. |
   428	| Terminal completeness | A settled root contains no unfinished initialization, memory operation, verifier job, adapter or set-union job. |
   429	
   430	The current Quint invariants reject duplicate accumulator certificate references and require all children to share the node’s program. Those clauses must change; retaining them would reject the required shared-ancestor DAG. [`models/sherlock/quint/aggregation.qnt:457`; `models/sherlock/quint/aggregation.qnt:464`]
   431	
   432	Keep the existing height arithmetic lemmas for owner steps. Do not introduce a 64-bit **global PCD depth** counter as an extra acceptance bound. Model finite DAG well-foundedness with natural-number ranks in the formal model. The cryptographic construction must separately justify extraction of a finite valid computation graph. [`clean/fork/Clean/Sherlock/Aggregation.lean:1185`; `D:general-purpose.md:44`]
   433	
   434	The key new refinement theorem should establish:
   435	
   436	> A decided root yields a well-founded graph of typed claims. Every Sherlock run has authenticated initialization and the claimed execution. Every foreign claim satisfies its registered verifier and adapter. Every dependency is authenticated. Every published claim-set entry has the claimed provenance.
   437	
   438	This theorem must retain explicit cryptographic premises. The current `leaf_sound` and `valid_accumulate` assumptions do not constitute proofs of the processor circuit or the accumulation scheme. [`clean/fork/Clean/Sherlock/Aggregation.lean:204`; `clean/fork/Clean/Sherlock/Aggregation.lean:1242`]
   439	
   440	### Constant-size interpretation
   441	
   442	The final verifier checks a fixed-width commitment to the graph and claim set. Opening or enumerating the entire set is a separate operation.
   443	
   444	If “knows exactly which claims” were interpreted as reading every claim during the constant-cost final verification, the requirements would conflict: the verifier cannot read an arbitrarily long list in constant work. The commitment-and-opening interpretation matches GP-2’s explicit requirement that individual claims can be opened. [`D:general-purpose.md:25`; `D:general-purpose.md:47`]
   445	
   446	## 5. On-chain fit
   447	
   448	Retain one Midnight-format outer proof. Replace the program-sealed contract with a dynamic settlement interface.
   449	
   450	A proposed interface binds:
   451	
   452	```text
   453	acceptRun(
   454	    request_d,
   455	    registry_policy_d,
   456	    claim_set_d,
   457	    shape_d,
   458	    settlement_pre_d,
   459	    settlement_post_d,
   460	    optional_single_run_fields
   461	)
   462	```
   463	
   464	The decider proves that these arguments describe its authenticated root. For a singleton, the fixed argument vector can include `prog_d`, `input_d`, outcome tag and `outcome_d`. For a batch, it exposes the claim-set commitment. Unused fields have canonical values.
   465	
   466	The contract checks the authorized registry policy and the current settlement root. It updates the settlement root only after proof verification. The proved transition inserts semantic nullifiers, so another shape or another batch grouping cannot replay an already consumed effect.
   467	
   468	The present contract seals `program_d` and `init_d` and stores a u128 result. Those choices must change. Its deployed operation must continue to use the decider key, rather than the compiled key of a template that only performs ledger operations. [`tools/sherlock-sdk/sherlock-run.compact:3`; `tools/sherlock-sdk/sherlock-run.compact:23`]
   469	
   470	The relevant ledger limits are:
   471	
   472	| Limit | Pinned source |
   473	|---|---|
   474	| Raw serialized verifier key: at most **50,000 bytes** | `L:transient-crypto/src/proofs.rs:422` |
   475	| Public inputs per call: at most **65,536 field elements** | `L:onchain-runtime/src/transcript.rs:87`; enforcement at `L:ledger/src/verify.rs:2138` |
   476	| Contract operations: at most **256** | `L:ledger/src/structure.rs:1928` |
   477	| Ordinary public-input vector length must match the key | `Z:lib.rs:2020` |
   478	
   479	Proposed interface target: **ESTIMATE fewer than 256 public-input elements**, including transcript overhead. The actual count must come from the recaptured Compact template.
   480	
   481	There is no demonstrated k=14 verifier ceiling. The KZG verifier parameter object holds G2 material rather than a k-sized commitment basis, the ledger verification function has no explicit k limit, and the recorded k=20 decider passes the installed ledger verifier. [`P:poly/kzg/params.rs:235`; `L:transient-crypto/src/proofs.rs:563`; `qa/sherlock/decisions/speed-levers.json:95`]
   482	
   483	Thus k=20 is a resource and architecture target here, not a source-proven ledger maximum. Increasing k still changes proving cost and SRS requirements and must not be presented as a free remedy.
   484	
   485	Full transaction validation remains necessary. The earlier review found that the GP harness exercised `VerifierKey::verify`, while the named independent `verify_chain` example still belonged to the historical aggregation route. A successful cryptographic verification alone does not close contract integration. [`research/sherlock-recursion-design-review-20261110/review.md:164`]
   486	
   487	## 6. Migration and milestones
   488	
   489	### Preserve
   490	
   491	- **Reference semantics and C6 rule reasoning.** Reuse them as the specification and differential oracle for the committed-memory interpreter. The existing full-carrier encoding is not the new memory architecture. [`D:design.md:27`; `crates/mori-backend/src/sherlock_c6_child.rs:551`]
   492	- **Height and genesis-carry hardening.** Preserve their obligations and negative cases, while adapting the accumulator representation and disabled-slot normalization. [`crates/mori-recursion/src/ivc.rs:497`; `crates/mori-recursion/src/ivc.rs:517`]
   493	- **Terminal pairing implementation.** Reuse its arithmetic and binding checks in the new decider. [`crates/mori-recursion/src/run_decider.rs:247`]
   494	- **Transcript-template machinery and local ledger verification harness.** Reuse the mechanism with a newly captured dynamic contract template. [`crates/mori-recursion/src/run_decider.rs:290`]
   495	- **Lean segment composition.** Keep it as a component of the typed PCD model. [`clean/fork/Clean/Sherlock/Aggregation.lean:958`]
   496	
   497	### Replace
   498	
   499	- `SHKIVC06` as the final processor: it has one predecessor and one fixed leaf key. [`crates/mori-recursion/src/ivc.rs:548`]
   500	- The monolithic, capacity-bound universal leaf as the sole route to GP-1. [`D:design.md:52`; `D:general-purpose.md:13`]
   501	- The fixed leaf-key field and foreign-proof assumptions.
   502	- The halted-u128-only decider boundary.
   503	- The program-sealed contract.
   504	- Global program fixity and no-duplicate-reference clauses in the aggregation model.
   505	- Off-chain-only graph-shape binding.
   506	
   507	### Proposed milestone order
   508	
   509	| Milestone | Working software delivered | Acceptance boundary |
   510	|---|---|---|
   511	| **M1: binary recursive kernel** | Two self slots, canonical disabled slots, a minimal inline transition, root accumulator and terminal decider. | Real proofs for base, unary and binary nodes under one node key. Tampered child, carry, key and shape refused through final verification. |
   512	| **M2: committed-memory processor** | Authenticated loads, stores, continuation boundaries and canonical initialization. | One program exceeds the resident carrier window without changing keys. Malformed paths and interrupted writes cannot publish a completed claim. |
   513	| **M3: complete Sherlock interpreter** | Program and input as data, every language rule, all outcome kinds and canonical structured values. | GP-1’s unseen-program acceptance suite passes under one frozen key set. |
   514	| **M4: typed composition** | Consume verified results, authenticate input mappings, build claim sets and support DAG reuse. | GP-2 and GP-4 tests, including depth-eight shapes and a shared producer, pass under the same keys. |
   515	| **M5: foreign-proof verification** | Registry admission, canonical VK interpretation, actual Compact Blake2b verification and statement adapters. | One proof combines a Sherlock run and a Compact contract proof, with both identities authenticated against the registry. |
   516	| **M6: settlement and performance** | Dynamic contract, semantic replay protection, independent bundle verification and full local call validation. | All four GP acceptance suites and the separately retained throughput requirements pass. |
   517	
   518	Develop the typed claim definitions and formal refinements alongside M1–M4. They must describe the implementation’s continuation and publication boundaries before that implementation is presented as a completed GP milestone.
   519	
   520	Do not freeze final keys after M1 merely because recursion works. Freeze after the processor architecture, complete instruction semantics, statement encoding and decider relation are stable. Subsequent application programs, registry entries and graph shapes must use those keys unchanged.
   521	
   522	### Proposed EARS requirements and design changes
   523	
   524	These are proposed text, not file edits.
   525	
   526	| Proposed requirement | Text |
   527	|---|---|
   528	| **GP-MEM** | The processor SHALL authenticate every program, input and mutable-memory access against committed storage. Storage capacity SHALL NOT depend on a proving key. |
   529	| **GP-INIT** | Before the processor publishes a run segment as originating at height zero, it SHALL prove canonical initialization from the committed program, input and execution parameters. |
   530	| **GP-FOREIGN** | When a foreign proof is consumed, the processor SHALL authenticate its complete verifier interpretation and statement adapter against an authorized registry commitment. |
   531	| **GP-FORMAT** | When a claim asserts compatibility with a ledger proof format, the processor SHALL enforce that format’s transcript, encodings, instance layout and complete parsing rules. |
   532	| **GP-SHAPE** | Every decided root SHALL bind the typed dependency graph and local work into its public statement. |
   533	| **GP-DAG** | The processor SHALL permit a verified claim to feed multiple consumers. It SHALL prevent that reuse from duplicating execution coverage or settlement effects. |
   534	| **GP-COMPLETE** | The decider SHALL refuse a root containing an unfinished initialization, memory, verifier, adapter or claim-set operation. |
   535	| **GP-KEYS** | Changing a program, input, memory capacity, registry entry, graph shape or graph depth SHALL NOT require a different processor or decider key. |
   536	
   537	Replace D4 and §§2.3–2.4 with the bounded processor and committed-memory design. Replace §3.1 with binary PCD. Extend §3.3 to typed terminal claims. Replace §3.6’s single-program segment model with typed composition while retaining segment linkage as a subrelation. Rewrite SRC-013/014 around processor work per proof, rather than a finite family or fixed-capacity carrier. [`D:design.md:12`; `D:design.md:46`; `D:design.md:70`; `D:design.md:114`; `D:requirements.md:35`]
   538	
   539	Two previous positions should not survive this change:
   540	
   541	1. Off-chain genesis recomputation cannot be the only authentication mechanism for claims consumed in-circuit.
   542	2. The failed three-proof node does not establish that every binary node with new work requires k=21.
   543	
   544	The first follows from the required consumption boundary. The second follows from the distinct two-self modeling result. [`D:general-purpose.md:24`; `qa/sherlock/decisions/speed-levers.json:26`]
   545	
   546	## 7. Ranked experiments
   547	
   548	All experiments below are future work. Costs are **ESTIMATE** for 20 cores, approximately 120 GB shared RAM and one heavy job at a time. They exclude implementation effort unless stated. A measurement that exceeds a cap is a failure, not permission to change the workload.
   549	
   550	First ingest the already-running per-rule, leaf-headroom, genesis and k=20 leaf results. Do not duplicate them. They inform the comparison with the present route, but do not measure the proposed binary processor. [`research/sherlock-gp-architecture-20261110/BRIEF.md:24`]
   551	
   552	| Rank | Hypothesis | Measurement | Kill criterion | Rough machine cost |
   553	|---|---|---|---|---|
   554	| **1. Binary node and decider closure** | Two self slots, canonical disabled encodings and useful inline work fit at k=20. | Model exact self architecture, final statement width, both active slots, base case and decider. Then prove base/unary/binary cases and run fresh invalid-child tests through the decider. | Node exceeds 996,147 rows with the minimum useful kernel, or decider exceeds its approved envelope. Any normalization soundness failure stops adoption. | **ESTIMATE:** 5–20 minutes and 2–5 GB for modeling; 15–45 minutes and 20–40 GB for a small proof closure. |
   555	| **2. Memory and initialization budget** | Committed-memory operations leave enough useful work per node to avoid excessive proof counts. | Rows per tree compression, read, write, page operation and continuation boundary. Test growing storage with identical keys. Include malformed genesis and partial-write publication. | Capacity changes keys; path length becomes a hidden admission cap; or measured work per node cannot support the throughput budget. | **ESTIMATE:** 10–40 minutes modeling; 30–90 minutes for selected proofs, 20–40 GB peak. |
   556	| **3. Actual Compact proof compatibility** | A processor implementation can reproduce ledger verification exactly. | Use a real ZKIR v3 contract proof. Compare transcript challenges, VK representative, PI vector, KZG reduction and acceptance with the pinned native verifier. Mutate every boundary, including trailing bytes. | Requires reissuing the proof under Poseidon, trusting a host verifier result, or changing keys for each foreign circuit. | **ESTIMATE:** 15–60 minutes for native differential work; 1–6 hours for the first segmented in-circuit proof, 20–40 GB peak. |
   557	| **4. Dynamic architecture and registry admission** | Different foreign architectures can be authenticated without changing `N` or `D`. | Admit keys with different chip sets, k, PI counts and verifier descriptors. Test descriptor/key mismatch, wrong adapter, unauthorized root and alternate SRS. | Any accepted mismatch, or a new architecture requires rebuilding the processor key. | **ESTIMATE:** 30–120 minutes for small modeled cases; several hours for proof-backed cases, depending on verifier trace length. |
   558	| **5. DAG and claim-set semantics** | Fan-out and exact coverage coexist without forbidding shared ancestors. | Extend Lean and Quint with typed claims and shared-node maps. Exercise diamond graphs, repeated references, conflicting identities, omitted union members and replay under another shape. | A shared ancestor is refused merely because it appears twice, or a reused ancestor duplicates an effect. | **ESTIMATE:** 5–30 minutes for bounded model runs; proof-backed minimal cases take 30–120 minutes. |
   559	| **6. Complete GP acceptance and throughput** | The same frozen keys satisfy all four GP tests at acceptable cost. | Unseen programs, oversized state, input, every outcome, cross-run consumption, mixed-program batch, Compact proof, chain/tree/DAG depth ≥8, equal terminal-proof lengths and local call validation. Record total kernel steps, proof count and wall time. | Any requirement needs a narrower profile or new application key. Existing two-hour workload target still fails if total measured wall exceeds it. | **ESTIMATE:** 8–16 hours for a straightforward full binary depth-eight suite on the KZG candidate. A perfect binary tree of that depth can require hundreds of node proofs. Peak 20–40 GB. |
